1074:
(a) No license for the sale of alcoholic beverages shall be granted to any person who is not a citizen of the United States or an alien lawfully admitted for permanent residence or otherwise authorized to work or conduct business in the United States pursuant to applicable federal law.
(b) Licenses under this chapter shall be issued by the director of finance or his designee. Licenses shall be issued only after the following has been determined:
(1) The applicant has not been convicted of a violation of any state or federal statute pertaining to any controlled substances;
(2) The applicant has not been convicted of a violation of any federal, state, county or city law or regulation regarding alcoholic beverages, their sale, distribution or manufacture within the past ten (10) years from the date of application;
(3) The applicant has not within the preceding ten (10) years had an alcoholic beverage or business license or permit suspended or revoked by the city, the State of Georgia, or any other government, state or political subdivision;
(4) The applicant has not in the past conducted any business within the city that has resulted in repeated or substantial violations of federal, state, or local law, or demonstrated a pattern of noncompliance with lawful governmental regulations;
(5) That the building or other facility where the alcoholic beverages are to be sold or dispensed meets all applicable city ordinances, building codes, fire codes, and similar such codes;
(6) The applicant will provide adequate parking spaces/facilities so as not to cause traffic congestion, unlawful parking or unauthorized parking on any nearby properties;
(7) The applicant shall owe no monies to the city, Clayton County, the State of Georgia, or the federal government that is delinquent;
(8) That applicant has obtained or will obtain a license in compliance with the laws and regulations of the State of Georgia concerning the sale, distribution, and consumption of alcoholic beverages;
(9) The proposed location shall meet all of the minimum distance requirements set forth in this chapter, as evidenced by a plat of survey prepared by a surveyor licensed and registered by the State of Georgia;
(10) In the case of the retail sale of malt beverages and/or wine by the package, the application shall contain a summary of the current inventory required by section 9-2-42 of this chapter;
(11) The applicant will ensure that all employees serving, dispensing, or handling alcohol will obtain a permit from the city. Such employees must provide valid identification, a Social Security card and complete a notarized criminal history consent form, authorizing the police department to conduct a complete criminal background investigation, with a fee as established by the governing body by resolution from time to time and kept on file in the office of the director of finance. The permit can be used at any applicable establishment without the need of obtaining a new permit;
(12) The applicant shall have complied with the requirements of section 9-2-31 of this chapter pertaining to the installation and maintenance of video cameras within the applicant's premises;
(13) The applicant has provided complete and accurate information in the application and has not made any material misrepresentation, omission, or false statement in connection with the application;
(14) The proposed operation will comply with all applicable provisions of this chapter, state law, zoning regulations, building codes, fire codes, and other applicable laws and regulations; and
(15) The applicant has designated a responsible individual authorized to act on behalf of the licensee regarding matters arising under this chapter.
(c) The chief of police or his designee shall cause an appropriate investigation to be made into the persons named and the information given on the application, and shall cause the fingerprinting process as required by O.C.G.A. § 3-3-2 to be completed and forwarded to the Georgia Bureau of Investigation. Upon completion of the investigation and based upon the criteria specified above, the chief of police or his designee shall approve or deny the application. The chief of police or his designee shall notify the applicant, within ten (10) days, of his decision.
(1) Upon approval by the chief of police or his designee and upon receipt of payment of the license fee, the director of finance shall issue the applicable license or licenses to the applicant. The amount of the license fee shall be established by the governing body from time to time in a separate ordinance.
(2) If the application is denied by the chief of police or his designee, such decision is subject to the right of appeal provided in section 9-2-19 of this chapter.
(Ord. No. 12-33, § 1, 9-17-2012; Ord. No. 14-23, § 1, 10-6-2014; Ord. No. 16-03, § 1, 4-4-2016; Ord. No. 22-12, § 10, 7-18-2022; Ord. No. 26-16, § 1(Exh. A), 8-3-2026)

1085:
(a) Any revocation by the State of Georgia of any permit or license issued to any licensees herein pertaining to the sale of alcoholic beverages shall automatically revoke any companion permit or license issued by the city, without any action required by the city, and shall not be appealable.
(b) Except as provided in subsection (a) above, no license issued hereunder shall be denied, suspended or revoked except for due cause as hereinafter set forth.
(c) Due cause defined. "Due cause" for the denial, suspension or revocation of any license issued under this chapter shall consist of:
(1) Any material misrepresentation, omission, or false statement in any application, renewal application, report, or other document submitted to the city;
(2) Failure to continuously satisfy the qualifications or standards required for issuance of the license;
(3) Violation of any provision of this chapter, any condition of a license issued under this chapter, or any applicable federal, state, or local law governing alcoholic beverages;
(4) Permitting or maintaining unlawful activity, including controlled substance offenses, gambling offenses, prostitution, human trafficking, or other criminal activity on or about the licensed premises;
(5) Repeated violations of federal, state, or local laws or regulations occurring on the licensed premises;
(6) Failure to cooperate with lawful inspections or investigations conducted by the city or other governmental authorities;
(7) Failure to pay taxes, fees, penalties, or other amounts lawfully owed to the city;
(8) Operating in a manner that constitutes a threat to the public health, safety, or welfare; or
(9) Any other ground that would have constituted a basis for denial of the original application.
(d) Hearings.
(1) No license issued under this article shall be denied, suspended or revoked without the opportunity for a hearing as hereinafter provided. This provision shall also apply to alcoholic beverage permits for employees.
(2) The director of finance or his designee shall provide written notice to the applicant or licensee of his action to deny, suspend or revoke the license. Such written notification shall set forth in reasonable detail the reasons for such action and shall notify the applicant or licensee of the right to appeal under the provisions of this chapter. Any applicant or licensee who is aggrieved or adversely affected by a final action of the director of finance may have a review thereof by appeal to the city manager or his designee (hereinafter called "the hearing officer"). Such appeal shall be by written petition, filed in the office of the director of finance within fifteen (15) days after the action of the director of finance and in order to defray administrative costs, must be accompanied by a filing fee of two hundred and fifty dollars ($250.00). The director of finance, at his discretion, may waive or reduce the filing fee amount if it is determined the fee would create a hardship on the individual filing said appeal. Failure to file the appeal within the established time period will result in the action of the director of finance being made final, and no further appeal shall be allowed.
(3) Upon a timely filed appeal, a hearing shall be conducted by the hearing officer on each appeal within thirty (30) days of the date of filing of the appeal with the director of finance, unless a continuance of such date is agreed to by the appellant and the director of finance. The hearing officer shall provide notice to all parties of the date, time and location of the hearing. At the hearing, the appellant shall show cause why the revocation or suspension should be reversed. The appellant shall have the right to appear at the hearing in person and to be represented by counsel (at his own expense), and shall have the right to present evidence and to cross-examine any witnesses who are called to testify on behalf of the city. At the hearing, the appellant shall show cause why the revocation or suspension should be reversed. Should the appellant desire an official transcript of the appeal proceedings, then such request must be made at least three (3) days prior to such hearing. The appellant shall have the burden of proof on any such appeal. The rules of evidence shall not apply to such a hearing, but the hearing shall be conducted in a fundamentally fair manner. Within fifteen (15) days following the conclusion of the hearing, the hearing officer shall issue a written decision including findings of fact, and may either (i) find that the evidence warrants the denial, suspension or revocation; or (ii) find that the evidence does not warrant the denial, suspension or revocation, and provide notice of his decision to the parties. The hearing officer may affirm, reverse, modify, or remand the decision under review and may impose reasonable conditions upon any continued licensure when authorized by this chapter.
(4) The findings of the hearing officer shall not be set aside unless found to be: (1) contrary to law or ordinances; or (2) unsupported by substantial evidence on the record as a whole; or (3) unreasonable.
(5) The findings of the hearing officer shall be final unless appealed within thirty (30) days of the date of said finding. Any applicant or appellant aggrieved by any decision made by any hearing officer pursuant to this section may file a petition for a writ of certiorari in the superior court to contest any such decision.
(6) Any person filing a petition for a writ of certiorari under this section shall pay fee in the amount of one thousand dollars ($1,000.00) to the city clerk to help defray the cost of assembling, preparation of the transcript, copying, and transmitting the record to the superior court.
(7) For the purpose of this chapter, notice shall be deemed delivered upon personal delivery, electronic transmission to the email address provided by the applicant or licensee, or three (3) days after deposit in the United States mail, postage prepaid, addressed to the last address provided to the city.
(Ord. No. 12-33, § 1, 9-17-2012; Ord. No. 26-16, § 1(Exh. A), 8-3-2026)

